The post-theorem introduction gives the proof structure for the main theorem as follows. First, the paper "remind[s] the factorization properties of the ring $\mathbb{R}[\cos t,\sin t]$" and then it "show[s] the characterization of the invariant curves of
\begin{equation}\label{ecu1}
		x' = A(t) x^3 + B(t) x^2 + C(t) x
	\end{equation}
." Next, assuming that "the above equation has two or more different invariant curves," the authors "prove (a) and (b.1)." Then, they "focus in the polynomial case to provide the general bound of (b.2.1)." Finally, they "apply Darboux's Theory of Integrability to prove the remaining parts of (b.2.1), together with (b.2.2) and (c)."

