ref_dict.example:sharpness:
\begin{example}\label{example:sharpness}
    Given an unramified regular local ring $(A,\mathfrak{m})$ of dimension $d>0$ and a positive integer $c\leq d$, there exists an ideal $I$ of $A$ such that $\cd(A,I)=d-\floor*{\frac{d-1}{c}}.$ We construct this ideal as in \cite{Lyu2}. 

    Let $N=\floor*{\frac{d-1}{c}}.$ Let $I_0,\dots,I_N$ be ideals of pure height $c$ in $A$ such that $I_0+\dots+I_N$ is $\mathfrak{m}$-primary and let $I=I_0\cap\dots\cap I_N$. Then $\cd(A,I)=d-N.$ The proof of this statement is identical to \cite{Lyu2}.

    To resolve any conflict with the previous remark, we show that in this example, if $p\in I$, then $\floor*{\frac{d-2}{c-1}}= \floor*{\frac{d-1}{c}}.$ Indeed, if $p\in I,$ we have $p\in I_j$ for all $j\in\{0,\dots,N\}$ and by Theorem \ref{thm:Serre}, $$d=\height(I_0+\dots+I_N)\leq 1+(N+1)(c-1)=Nc+c-N.$$ Let $d-1=Nc+k$, where $0\leq k<c$. Then the above equation yields $N+k+1\leq c$, or $N+k-1\leq c-2$. Observe that $$d-2=N(c-1)+N+k-1,$$ and hence, $\floor*{\frac{d-2}{c-1}}=N=\floor*{\frac{d-1}{c}}$. 
\end{example}

ref_dict.thm:induction:
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:induction}
    Let $(A,\mathfrak{m},k)$ be an unramified regular local ring of mixed characteristic $(0,p)$, and let $I$ be an ideal of $A$. Suppose $\height(I)<\height(I:(I:p))$ (for example, this holds if $p$ is a nonzerodivisor on $A/I)$.\footnote{We use the convention that $\height(A)=\infty.$}

    Set $c=\bight(I)$. Let $n>c$ be an integer. Assume that for all integers $s$, with $1\leq s\leq c-1,$ and for all $q\geq n-s$, $H^q_{IA_P}(A_P)=0$ for all $P\in \Spec(A)$ such that $I\subseteq P$ and $\dim(A/P)\geq s+1.$ Then $H^q_I(A)=0$ for all $q\geq n.$
\end{theorem}

expanded_sketch:
We split our proof of Theorem \ref{thm:maintheorem} into three cases.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $p$ is a nonzerodivisor on $A/I$, we use the following inductive criterion to prove that the local cohomology modules $H_I^n(A)$ vanish for $n$ greater than the desired bound.

\begin{maintheorem}\label{thm:indcrit}(cf. Theorem \ref{thm:induction})
    Let $(A,\mathfrak{m})$ be an unramified regular local ring of mixed characteristic $(0,p)$ and $I$ be an ideal of $A$ of big height $c$. Suppose $p$ is a nonzerodivisor on $A/I.$

    Let $n>c$ be an integer. Assume that for all integers $s$, with $1\leq s\leq c-1,$ and for all $q\geq n-s$, $H^q_{IA_P}(A_P)=0$ for all $P\in \Spec(A)$ such that $I\subseteq P$ and $\dim(A/P)\geq s+1.$ Then $H^q_I(A)=0$ for all $q\geq n.$
\end{maintheorem}

\item If $p\in I$, we reduce our analysis to $A/p$, a regular local ring of equal characteristic $p$, and employ the already known bound in equal characteristic.
\item If $p$ is a zerodivisor on $I$, we first use the following lemma to reduce again to equal characteristic $p$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:p_zd_on_H_I^n}
    Let $I$, $A$, $x$, $I_1$ and $I_2$ be as in Lemma \ref{lemma:radical(I+p)}, $M$ be an $A$-module and $n>\max(\cd(M,I_1),\cd(M,I_2))$. Then $H_I^n(M)$ is $x$-power torsion, that is, every element of $H^n_I(M)$ is annihilated by some power of $x$.
\end{lemma}

One then refines the argument to control the behavior of $\operatorname{bight}(I(A/p))$; all cases are resolved except when $\operatorname{bight}(I)$ divides $\dim(A)-1$. In this remaining case, one uses the structural description of the critical local cohomology module $H^q_J(A/p)$ \cite[Theorem 4.1]{Lyu1} to reduce to the previously treated situation in which $p$ is a nonzerodivisor on $A/I$.
\end{enumerate}
Additionally, the inductive criterion itself is proved by generalizing Faltings' criterion to unramified mixed characteristic (assuming $p$ is a nonzerodivisor on $A/I$): one reduces to local cohomology supported only at the maximal ideal, introduces an auxiliary tensor construction over the base discrete valuation ring and analyzes local cohomology with respect to the diagonal ideal, using dimension bounds/numerical inequalities to force the collapse of a natural spectral sequence and obtain vanishing; then one leverages Zhou's result on injective dimension \cite[Theorem 5.1]{Zhou} together with finiteness of Bass numbers \cite{Lyu4, NB}.

