theorem.content:
\label{T1}

Let $(\Omega,g)$ be a Jordan domain with boundary $\partial \Omega =\Sigma$. We choose the orthornomal frame $\{e_1,e_2\}$ so that $e_1$ is tangent to $\Sigma$ and $e_2$ is normal to $\Sigma$. The boundary curve $\Sigma$ is parameterised as
\begin{equation}
[0,2\pi) \ni \varphi \mapsto i(\varphi) \in \Sigma. \end{equation}
Denote by $L=2\pi r_0$ and $k=g(\nabla^g_{e_1}e_2,e_1)$ the length and the geodesic curvature of $\Sigma$ with respect to the outward normal $e_2$ of $\Sigma$ respectively. We can isometrically embed the boundary curve $\Sigma$ as the round circle $F(\Sigma)=\{r=r_0\}$ into the standard hyperbolic 2-space $(\mathbb{H}^2,g_{-1}=\frac{\mathrm{d} r^2}{1+r^2}+r^2\mathrm{d} \varphi^2)$ by identifying the angle parameter $\varphi$. Denote by $\hat k$ the geodesic curvature of the embedded round circle $F(\Sigma)$ in $\mathbb{H}^2$. Suppose that $K_g \geq -1$ in $\Omega$ and $k>0$ on $\Sigma$. Then one has
\begin{equation}\label{MT}
\int_\Sigma k  \mathrm{d} s \leq \int_\Sigma \hat k \mathrm{d} s= 2\pi \sqrt{1+r_0^2}
\end{equation}
Moreover, if the equality holds, then $(\Omega,g)$ must be a geodesic disk in the standard hyperbolic 2-space $(\mathbb{H}^2,g_{-1})$.

