post_theorem_intro_text:
\begin{proof}
The $\F_3$ lines through the $N(N-1)/2$ pairs of points of the capset have their third point in the complement of the capset, so if $N(N-1)/2 < 3^n -N$, there is a point in the complement not on any line through two points in the capset, hence the capset is not complete. 
\end{proof}
In \cite{Csajbok}, subsets of $\F_p^n$ with $p$ prime no three points in arithmetic progression are studied. This condition is equivalent to the capset condition when $p=3$. They prove a lower bound similar, but slightly weaker, to Lemma \ref{lem:lower} in this more general situation. They also ask if this lower bound is best possible up to a multiplicative constant (loc. cit., Problem 5.1). We give a positive answer to this question in the case $p=3$ (see Corollary \ref{cor:upper}).

